  "body": {
    "id": "lem:primary-morse-nonlinear-incompressible-rigidity",
    "kind": "lemma",
    "statement": "For every aligned pair in \\(\\mathcal M^{\\mathrm{Morse}}_{2,2,1}(c_0,C_0,K,a_0,J,\\Delta_0)\\), the determinant residual and two Morse ratio residuals satisfy\n\\[\n d_Q(\\theta,\\widetilde\\theta)\n \\leq C\\mathcal E(\\theta,\\widetilde\\theta)^{3/4}.\n\\]\nAfter removal of coordinatewise fiber means, the mixed displacement obeys\n\\[\n \\lVert v_{\\rm mix}\\rVert_2\\leq C\\{\\mathcal E/h+h^3\\}.\n\\]\nThus \\(h=\\mathcal E^{1/4}\\) gives the exponent \\(3/4\\), uniformly over arbitrary bounded \\(C^2\\) torus diffeomorphisms in the primary class.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "theta",
      "theta_tilde",
      "primary_morse_class",
      "c0",
      "C0",
      "K",
      "a0",
      "J",
      "Delta0",
      "residual",
      "intrinsic_quotient_distance",
      "n",
      "k",
      "m"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "ass:primary-morse-hessian",
      "ass:finite-type-profile",
      "def:primary-morse-class",
      "lem:primary-morse-weighted-divfree-coercivity",
      "lem:finite-type-incompressible-rigidity"
    ],
    "proof_tex": "The primary Hessian assumption and Taylor's formula give, near each critical point,\n\\[\n |R_i'(z+u)|\\geq(a_0/2)|u|.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\nAway from those neighborhoods, the finite-type profile gives the same form after changing the constant.  Lemma \\(\\mathrm{lem:finite\\text{-}type\\text{-}incompressible\\text{-}rigidity}\\), with \\(n=2,k=2,m=1\\), therefore yields\n\\[\n \\lVert v_{\\rm mix}\\rVert_2\\leq C\\{\\mathcal E/h+h^3\\}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\nIts tangent coefficient system is the arbitrary-field system proved in Lemma \\(\\mathrm{lem:primary\\text{-}morse\\text{-}weighted\\text{-}divfree\\text{-}coercivity}\\), and its nonlinear proof retains \\(\\det DT-1\\), rather than asserting \\(\\operatorname {div}v=0\\).\n\nFor \\(0<\\mathcal E\\leq h_0^4\\), take \\(h=\\mathcal E^{1/4}\\).  Then\n\\[\n \\mathcal E/h=h^3=\\mathcal E^{3/4}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\nThe comparison with both directed canonical losses proves the result.  Passage to the limit handles \\(\\mathcal E=0\\); bounded torus diameter handles the remaining fixed range.",
